# grep(1)

## NAME

grep - print lines that match patterns

## SYNOPSIS

    grep [-EFcHhilnqsvx] [-e pattern]... [pattern] [file...]

## DESCRIPTION

Prints the lines of the files (or standard input) that match a pattern. Patterns are basic regular expressions by default. Supported: `.` `[...]` `[:class:]` `*` `^` `$`, and `+ ?` (ERE) or `\+ \?` (BRE). Groups, alternation, intervals and back references are not supported.

A line is at most 64 KiB; longer lines are split.

## OPTIONS

- `-E`: extended regular expressions
- `-F`: fixed strings
- `-e pattern`: use this pattern (may be repeated)
- `-i`: ignore case
- `-v`: select non-matching lines
- `-x`: match whole lines only
- `-c`: print only a count of matching lines
- `-l`: print only the names of matching files
- `-q`: print nothing
- `-n`: prefix line numbers
- `-H`: prefix file names
- `-h`: never prefix file names
- `-s`: do not report unreadable files

## EXIT STATUS

- `0`: a line matched
- `1`: no line matched
- `2`: an error occurred

## EXAMPLES

    ls /bin | grep -c .

## SEE ALSO

sort(1), wc(1)
